{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794329710,"nonce":"4czpvlUTUvjeJed+upVOBQ/R+mg=","message":"Fallback tiers started with sdf_shadow (09993bd0ff703bcf32648d1eee949430b855a2d7). The three tiers are plain Rust modules (sdf_russl/{storage,vertex_buffer,data_texture}) that include the same shared sources with #[path]; each tier has its own access module (buffers.rs, textures.rs) giving prim(i) and aux(i), so a shader written once reads storage buffers or RGBA32F textures. The vertex stage differs only in its entry point (storage reads PRIMITIVES, the fallbacks read vertex attributes); the stage body is shared (shadow_vertex, shadow_fragment). This needed russl 0.3: a module's source macro is now local to its Rust module (russl da7b39d), so identical files under different modules no longer collide; #![export] keeps the old crate-root export for cross-crate imports. Verified: the russl _vb and _dt builds matched the hand-written WGSL pixel for pixel over all four shadow types, a rounded clip and a polygon clip read from aux data at index 1600 (the second texture row; shrinking the row width in the accessor fails the test), then the hand-written files were deleted and the tiers are compared with the storage tier. Next: sdf_core, sdf_3d and sdf_notch (their vertex stages differ more: core has a mesh path through aux in the vertex stage on the storage tier only), then sdf_simple and the non-SDF shaders. Not tested: the data texture tier on WebGL2 (GLES); native wgpu draws it.","files":null}]}